    "passage": "“You’d better watch out. You’d better not cry…” A tune should start playing in your head by now. [[1]] the chorus follows, “Santa Claus is coming to town.” So [[2]] the Christmas jingle known to every kid. We grew up singing that Santa would be “making a list and checking it twice” because he’s “gonna find out who’s naughty and nice.” So the best way to get children to be good at Christmas seems to be promising them presents. But in parts of Europe, some parents actually threaten the little ones with a half-demon, half-goat creature if they [[3]].\nMeet Krampus. He is believed to be the devilish [[4]] of St. Nicholas. The latter is the European [[5]] of Santa, and the pair traditionally visit kids on December 5th. The name Krampus [[6]] from the German word “krampen,” meaning “claw.” [[7]] St. Nicholas rewards good kids at Christmas, legend has it that Krampus punishes bad ones. He brings hot coal and bunches of birch twigs, which he beats the children with. In some cases, he is said to eat the particularly naughty ones.\nThe legend of the Krampus dates back to the 12th century. Despite the Catholic Church’s effort to [[8]] him during the Renaissance, Krampus became an integral part of German Christmas. Nowadays, many European countries like Austria, Germany, and the Czech Republic hold festivals to celebrate him. Recently, the Krampus fad has caught on in other parts of the world; no fewer than ten Krampus-theme parties were held in Tokyo in 2022 [[9]]. It’s even made it to the cinema, with Krampus, a 2015 horror comedy film, bringing good fun for fans of non-traditional holiday horror. Scary as Krampus may look, he actually [[10]] kids to be good. So who would you rather see coming down the chimney? Krampus, or Santa?",
